# Line endings, and why this file has to exist.
#
# vcpkg's own tooling writes LF. `vcpkg format-manifest` emits LF unconditionally,
# and `vcpkg x-add-version` refuses to process a port whose vcpkg.json does not
# match what format-manifest would produce:
#
#     error: logutil is not properly formatted
#     Run `vcpkg format-manifest ...` to format the file
#
# Every port manifest in this registry was CRLF - in the WORKING TREE and in the
# committed blob - because the registry had no .gitattributes and a Windows clone
# defaults to core.autocrlf=true. The content was already correct; the only
# difference from format-manifest's output was the line endings, byte for byte:
#
#     {  \r  \n    "  n  a  m  e  " ...      <- committed
#     {  \n        "  n  a  m  e  " ...      <- format-manifest
#
# WHAT THAT COST, and why it is worth a file rather than a note. The documented
# way to update this registry is
#
#     vcpkg x-add-version --all
#
# (see the tutorial at learn.microsoft.com/vcpkg/produce/publish-to-a-git-registry).
# With the manifests CRLF, `--all` SKIPS every port it considers unformatted and
# then rewrites versions/baseline.json with only the ports it processed - silently
# deleting the other thirteen. Run once, it removed envutil, fileutil, fpsutil,
# framestatutil, frameutil, logutil, magic-enum, processutil, queueutil, sortcpp,
# uriparseutil, vrtc-vrpc and vtpl-dependency-floor from the baseline. Every
# consumer of those ports would have stopped resolving.
#
# So the recommended command was unusable here, and the reason was invisible:
# nothing about a line ending appears in `git status`, or in a diff, or in the
# error message, which says "not properly formatted" and means "not LF".
#
# eol=lf pins the WORKING TREE and not just the blob, so a Windows clone gets the
# same bytes vcpkg's tools expect to find.
* text=auto

*.json    text eol=lf
*.cmake   text eol=lf
*.in      text eol=lf
usage     text eol=lf
*.patch   text eol=lf
*.sh      text eol=lf
